@@ -1276,7 +1276,7 @@

 When a multi-microphone terminal combines a spatial beamformer (Chapter 5) with an echo canceller, three classical cascade orders exist for answering "AEC first or beamformer first?":

-1. **AEC $\to$ BF (per-channel independent AEC)**: Run one linear AEC on each of the $M$ raw microphone channels, stripping echo before passing clean residuals to DOA estimation and beamforming. Its decisive advantage is that each channel's physical echo path $h_m[\ell]$ depends solely on the loudspeaker and room acoustics, completely decoupled from abrupt adaptive beam-weight updates; the cost is linear AEC compute and history memory scaling with $M$ (mitigated by shared reference FFTs below). Whenever DSP compute and memory permit and the back-end uses time-varying adaptive beams (MVDR, GSC, or steered tracking beams), AEC $\to$ BF is the preferred robust industrial architecture. Critically, when integrating monolithic AEC engines (e.g., WebRTC `ProcessStream()` or SpeexDSP) upstream of multichannel WPE or beamforming, feed forward the linear residual tap (`GetLinearAecOutput()`)—not the post-RES/NLP output—and side-chain the residual-echo PSD to post-beamformer RES/NLP to avoid temporal prediction mismatch from varying gains; channel-dependent processing may also alter spatial RTFs, whereas a common nonzero real gain preserves same-frame channel ratios and spatial phase (see [Chapter 7, §7.6](07_wpe-dereverberation.md#sec-7-6));
+1. **AEC $\to$ BF (per-channel independent AEC)**: Run one linear AEC on each of the $M$ raw microphone channels, stripping echo before passing clean residuals to DOA estimation and beamforming. Its decisive advantage is that each channel's physical echo path $h_m[\ell]$ depends solely on the loudspeaker and room acoustics, completely decoupled from abrupt adaptive beam-weight updates; the cost is linear AEC compute and history memory scaling with $M$ (mitigated by shared reference FFTs below). Whenever DSP compute and memory permit and the back-end uses time-varying adaptive beams (MVDR, GSC, or steered tracking beams), AEC $\to$ BF is the preferred robust industrial architecture. Critically, when integrating monolithic AEC engines (e.g., WebRTC `ProcessStream()` or SpeexDSP) upstream of multichannel WPE or beamforming, feed forward the linear residual tap (`GetLinearAecOutput()`)—not the post-RES/NLP output—and side-chain the residual-echo PSD to post-beamformer RES/NLP to avoid temporal prediction mismatch from varying gains; channel-dependent processing may also alter spatial RTFs, whereas a common nonzero real gain applied to all channel spectra at the same frame and frequency bin preserves their ratios and spatial phase (see [Chapter 7, §7.6](07_wpe-dereverberation.md#sec-7-6));
 2. **BF $\to$ AEC (single post-beamformer AEC)**: Combine the $M$ microphones into one beamformed output first, then run a single-channel linear AEC. Under the narrowband same-bin approximation (ignoring cross-band coupling), denoting microphone $m$'s echo transfer function at bin $k$, frame $n$ by $H_m(k,n)$ and its frequency-domain beam weight by $w_m(k,n)$ (forming $\sum_m w_m^* D_m$ per Chapter 5's conjugate inner product), the equivalent echo transfer function $H_{\mathrm{eff}}(k,n)$ seen by the post-beamformer AEC is (omitting shared bin/frame arguments):

     $$H_{\mathrm{eff}}\approx\sum_{m=1}^{M}w_m^*H_m.\tag{6-31}$$
