   1. **Conservative vs. agile gain split**: The foreground `refined` filter uses conservative error-spectrum-regulated gains that clamp tightly during double-talk to protect near-end speech; the shadow `coarse` filter uses aggressive NLMS-like steps to probe path jumps quickly;
   2. **Forward promotion on path jumps**: After a path jump, once the shadow filter sustains markedly lower residuals than the foreground during active far-end speech with sufficient coherence, the engine smoothly transitions output and copies shadow weights onto the foreground;
   3. **Reverse reset on double-talk divergence**: When heavy double-talk pushes the aggressive shadow filter into divergence with residuals worse than the foreground, the stable foreground weights overwrite and reset the shadow filter (three-state gating and double-talk overfitting rejection verified in Exercise [E06-30](#e06-30), Part 3); playback or capture saturation tightens both gains together;
-- **Residual-echo coupling (`ErleEstimator` $\to$ `ResidualEchoEstimator`) and dual-probe API**: Downstream of linear subtraction, `SubbandErleEstimator` and `FullBandErleEstimator` track per-band and fullband linear attenuation and feed `ResidualEchoEstimator`—which combines linear-residual scaling $|\hat Y|^2/\widehat{\mathrm{ERLE}}$, an exponential late-reverberation tail model (`ReverbModel`), and nonlinear headroom factors under playback saturation—before driving the frequency-domain suppressor and CNG. In WebRTC APM ([`audio_processing.h`](https://webrtc.googlesource.com/src/+/0467d2b91cc20b9b001c2bbb73d43ea6b2491f3e/api/audio/audio_processing.h)), every $10\ \mathrm{ms}$ tick must call `ProcessReverseStream()`, then `set_stream_delay_ms()` (out-of-range inputs clamp to $0$–$500\ \mathrm{ms}$), and finally `ProcessStream()`. During integration, always enable `export_linear_aec_output` and record `GetLinearAecOutput()` alongside final audio rather than tuning solely on the post-NLP output. Likewise, when placing monolithic engines such as WebRTC `ProcessStream()` or SpeexDSP upstream of multichannel WPE or beamforming, feed forward the linear residual tap (`GetLinearAecOutput()`)—not the post-RES/NLP output—and side-chain the residual-echo PSD to post-beamformer RES/NLP to avoid temporal prediction mismatch from varying gains; channel-dependent processing may also alter spatial RTFs, whereas a common nonzero real gain preserves same-frame channel ratios and spatial phase (see [Chapter 7, §7.6](07_wpe-dereverberation.md#sec-7-6)).
+- **Residual-echo coupling (`ErleEstimator` $\to$ `ResidualEchoEstimator`) and dual-probe API**: Downstream of linear subtraction, `SubbandErleEstimator` and `FullBandErleEstimator` track per-band and fullband linear attenuation and feed `ResidualEchoEstimator`—which combines linear-residual scaling $|\hat Y|^2/\widehat{\mathrm{ERLE}}$, an exponential late-reverberation tail model (`ReverbModel`), and nonlinear headroom factors under playback saturation—before driving the frequency-domain suppressor and CNG. In WebRTC APM ([`audio_processing.h`](https://webrtc.googlesource.com/src/+/0467d2b91cc20b9b001c2bbb73d43ea6b2491f3e/api/audio/audio_processing.h)), every $10\ \mathrm{ms}$ tick must call `ProcessReverseStream()`, then `set_stream_delay_ms()` (out-of-range inputs clamp to $0$–$500\ \mathrm{ms}$), and finally `ProcessStream()`. During integration, always enable `export_linear_aec_output` and record `GetLinearAecOutput()` alongside final audio rather than tuning solely on the post-NLP output. Likewise, when placing monolithic engines such as WebRTC `ProcessStream()` or SpeexDSP upstream of multichannel WPE or beamforming, feed forward the linear residual tap (`GetLinearAecOutput()`)—not the post-RES/NLP output—and side-chain the residual-echo PSD to post-beamformer RES/NLP to avoid temporal prediction mismatch from varying gains; channel-dependent processing may also alter spatial RTFs, whereas a common nonzero real gain applied to all channel spectra at the same frame and frequency bin preserves their ratios and spatial phase (see [Chapter 7, §7.6](07_wpe-dereverberation.md#sec-7-6)).

 **SpeexDSP architecture and asynchronous underrun false failures.** [SpeexDSP's `mdf.c`](https://gitlab.xiph.org/xiph/speexdsp/-/blob/8e29a256ef0235ebbe7fcb8417b5ac7731eb8307/libspeexdsp/mdf.c) runs Alternating Unconstrained MDF (AUMDF) paired with coherence-driven continuous learning-rate control (including built-in DC-notch high-pass filtering and pre-/de-emphasis; post-suppression requires binding a separate preprocessor via `SPEEX_PREPROCESS_SET_ECHO_STATE`). Integrating its asynchronous wrappers `speex_echo_playback()` / `speex_echo_capture()` frequently triggers a subtle false failure: if capture callbacks start ahead of playback callbacks, or playback thread jitter causes queue underruns, the async wrapper silently bypasses raw uncancelled microphone frames straight through (moreover, the async wrapper queues single-channel frame lengths and does not support interleaved multichannel streams). Always verify callback ordering, underrun counters, and channel mapping by injecting **staggered-timestamp** single impulses into each physical speaker channel during bring-up.
